--- body ---

## Context

Ticket 0393 built consumer-side keystore resolution in
`skills/reviewers/reviewers.sh` (`_keystore_file_for`, `_keystore_value`,
`_resolve_seat_credential`). Tracker 0942 then promoted that pattern to two more
consumers, deliberately duplicating a ~15-line idiom rather than extracting a
shared library — the rule of three was unmet and one port is Python.

The review panels on both ports found defects in the pattern itself, and each
port fixed them locally. The reference did not move. **It is now the worst of
the three copies**, and it is the one a future consumer will be told to copy.

This ticket is not "propagate a fix from the reference to its copies". It is the
reverse: bring back into 0393 what its descendants learned, and decide whether
three implementations should converge or whether the assumed duplication has a
bill now due.

## The defect table, as established by the round-1 and round-2 panels

| # | Defect | reviewers.sh | peer_review.py | resolve_hal_credentials.sh |
|---|--------|--------------|----------------|----------------------------|
| 1 | CRLF not stripped; `\r` embedded in the resolved value | present | fixed (trailing run only) | line added, NOT covered by any fixture |
| 2 | Provider file ending on a failing command reported as "unreadable" | present | fixed (`[ -r ]` probe) | to verify |
| 3 | No size or time cap on extraction: a FIFO hangs forever, ~3MB hangs >15s | present | present | present |
| 4 | Ambient env var names the file to be sourced | `REVIEWERS_KEYSTORE`, present | n/a (fixed path, test overrides HOME) | fixed (positional argument) |
| 5 | Name validation accepts any shell identifier, not the documented set | present | present | present |
| 6 | `UnicodeDecodeError` uncaught; `repr()` would embed the whole credential | n/a (bash) | fixed | n/a (bash) |

Defect 3 is the sharpest illustration: `scripts/bash-env.sh` carries an explicit
256 KiB cap and documents it. `reviewers.sh` never inherited it, so neither did
the two ports. The same is true of CRLF stripping, which `bash-env.sh` does and
documents. **The reference diverged from its own sibling before it was ever
copied**, and the wave multiplied the divergence by three.

Defect 4 is the only one that was exploitable. A project `.env` — a file
`bash-env.sh`'s own documentation says "an agent, a cloned repo, or any project
write can place" — could set `HAL_KEYSTORE_FILE` and redirect the resolver to an
attacker file that both returns spoofed values indistinguishable from real ones
and executes arbitrary code, since the file is sourced. Reproduced during the
0944 red-team pass. `REVIEWERS_KEYSTORE` is the same shape and is still open.

## What this ticket must decide, not just fix

Converging the three copies is not obviously right. The Imagine passes refused a
shared library for defensible reasons: the rule of three was unmet, one consumer
is Python, and extraction would have pushed each child ticket into
`reviewers.sh`, outside its exit criteria. Those reasons still hold. What has
changed is the evidence: a duplicated idiom drifted in three directions within
one working day, and the panels caught it only because three independent
red-team passes ran over the same week.

So the real question is whether the duplication is cheap because the idiom is
small, or expensive because it is security-relevant and its correctness is not
local. Answer that before writing code.

## Round-3 additions (2026-09-16, after the table above was drafted)

Ticket **0946** was filed by the 0943 executor and already covers defect 2 for
`reviewers.sh`. Do not duplicate it; reference it.

Defect 1's coverage gap in `resolve_hal_credentials.sh` was CLOSED during round
3 — the CR-strip line now has a fixture, and mutation testing killed all five of
that script's new guards. The table's third column is stale in its favour.

Three further findings, all reproduced by the round-3 red team, all requiring an
already-hostile calling environment (the same trust tier as the standing
"`$HOME` is honest" caveat), none a hole this wave opened:

- **7. The outer resolver script honours an ambient `BASH_ENV`**, so arbitrary
  code runs before its first line. Worth recording for the irony as much as the
  risk: a script written to stop credentials living in the environment is itself
  launched through the mechanism 0945 deletes. Closes with `unset -v BASH_ENV ENV`.
- **8. The 256 KiB cap calls `wc` through an uncontrolled `PATH`.** A planted
  `wc` let a 300 KB file through the cap. Closes with an absolute path, or by
  not shelling out to measure a size.
- **9. An array-valued keystore entry** (`HAL_PASSWORD=(a b)`) is silently
  accepted and truncated to its first element instead of failing loud.

## The finding that no reviewer could have made

Twenty-one review agents ran over the two PRs. Between them they found a
keystore-redirection hole, an inert test, a newline injection into a live
authenticated request, a missing size cap and a dormant `repr()` disclosure —
none of which any exit criterion had predicted.

The defect that would actually have shipped escaped all twenty-one: a
module-level `from openai import ...` made `peer_review.py` unimportable
wherever the SDK is absent, so seven credential-resolution tests passed locally
and failed in CI. Every reviewer worked inside the author's worktree, on the
author's machine, where `openai` is installed. The harness decorrelates
reviewers by MODEL and by ANGLE. It does not decorrelate them by ENVIRONMENT,
and a shared environment hides a defect from every agent in it at once.

CI was the only participant that did not share it. That is an argument for
treating the runner as a panel member rather than a gate, and for at least one
reviewer per wave to work from a clean checkout rather than the author's tree.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Defect 4 closed in `reviewers.sh`: `REVIEWERS_KEYSTORE` no longer lets an
      ambient variable name a file that will be sourced
- [ ] Defects 1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9 resolved in `reviewers.sh`, or explicitly
      declined per defect with a recorded reason (defect 2 belongs to 0946)
- [ ] Defect 3: the 256 KiB cap and its rationale are either inherited from
      `bash-env.sh` in all three, or the divergence is documented where a future
      copier will read it
- [ ] A decision is recorded, with its reasoning, on convergence versus
      continued duplication -- and if duplication stands, the reference file
      says so and says what a copier must check
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Notes
- Also out there, found by the 0943 consistency pass and NOT covered here:
  `scripts/zotero-import.py:940-949` hand-parses `~/.config/keys/zotero.env`
  instead of sourcing it. It resolves at point of use already, so it never
  showed up in 0942's sweep for ambient readers -- the sweep's population was
  "who reads the environment", not "who reads the keystore".
- The `exit N`-in-provider-file case (a correctly-set variable lost when the
  file exits explicitly) is present in all three and was ruled pre-existing and
  out of scope by two panels. Decide here whether it stays that way.
